Sure! To use the `prioritizeAndSort` helper, you’ll need to supply your array of objects (`myItemList`) as a JSON‑encoded string. Here’s a quick example of how you could call it:

```javascript
// Assume this is your original list:
const myItemList = [
  { name: 'Alpha', status: 'normal' },
  { name: 'Bravo', status: 'urgent' },
  { name: 'Charlie', status: 'normal' },
  { name: 'Delta', status: 'urgent' }
];

// Convert the array to a JSON string (the function expects a string)
const itemsJson = JSON.stringify(myItemList);

// Call the helper
const result = prioritizeAndSort({
  items: itemsJson,
  priorityStatus: 'urgent',
  ascending: true
});

// The helper will return the sorted array
console.log(result);
```

If you run this, the output will be:

```json
[
  { "name": "Bravo", "status": "urgent" },
  { "name": "Delta", "status": "urgent" },
  { "name": "Alpha", "status": "normal" },
  { "name": "Charlie", "status": "normal" }
]
```

So all “urgent” items appear first (in the order they were originally found), followed by the rest sorted alphabetically in ascending order.

